 and from then on, the English, they knew they had a war on their heads with these renegades in rags.

26
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:09,320
 Meanwhile, a Continental Congress was in session at Philadelphia.

27
00:07:10,530 --> 00:07:12,200
 We must make peace with England.

28
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:14,840
 We have no army, no money, and we have no leader.

29
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:17,260
 We took a hundred cannon at Ticonderoga.

30
00:07:17,420 --> 00:07:21,520
 And don't forget the Battle of Bunker Hill. Our patriots fought like heroes there.

31
00:07:23,210 --> 00:07:28,220
 Among us, we have a man who has already proved his bravery in battle.

32
00:07:28,590 --> 00:07:30,970
 He is the one who could lead us, Colonel George Washington.

33
00:07:30,970 --> 00:07:35,470
 I move that George Washington be named Commander-in-Chief over the whole Continental Army.

34
00:07:35,670 --> 00:07:36,930
 Those who agree vote aye.

35
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:44,290
 I may not be up to the noble task,

36
00:07:44,370 --> 00:07:46,750
 but I will carry out my duties to the best of my ability.

37
00:07:46,930 --> 00:07:48,960
 To this I pledge my life, my fortune, and my sacred honor.

38
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:53,900
 Washington was soon to discover that his job wasn't going to be an easy one.

39
00:07:54,670 --> 00:07:56,710
 And are we 20,000?

40
00:07:56,850 --> 00:07:58,290
 We're 14,000 men, General.

41
00:07:58,390 --> 00:07:59,670
 500 barrels of powder?

42
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:01,420
 No, General, 38.

43
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:04,200
 All my Connecticut soldiers have shown how brave they are.

44
00:08:04,260 --> 00:08:07,140
 May I request my men not be mixed in with those from Massachusetts?

45
00:08:07,140 --> 00:08:09,160
 Mass soldiers are ready to do battle with the English,

46
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:10,500
 but back home in Maryland,

47
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:14,460
 Not in this godforsaken backwater, primitive no-man's land, General Washington.

48
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:17,160
 Back in New Hampshire, we got brilliant officers,

49
00:08:17,300 --> 00:08:20,250
 and we got plenty enough to command the whole troops of the Army.

50
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:25,380
 Why or just how you became an officer?

51
00:08:25,520 --> 00:08:29,800
 Well, General, I'm an officer because my guys all decided that I should be their leader

52
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:32,140
 because it's me who gives the orders best.

53
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:34,420
 It was all decided fair and square, believe me.

54
00:08:35,710 --> 00:08:44,700
 Soldiers are supposed to do battle with the English, not with each other.

55
00:08:46,350 --> 00:08:48,250
 I'm the one who orders my soldiers.

56
00:08:48,390 --> 00:08:49,730
 No one else but me says fire.

57
00:08:49,930 --> 00:08:51,590
 They obey me, General. Nobody else.

58
00:08:51,770 --> 00:08:52,670
 You're wrong, Colonel.

59
00:08:52,870 --> 00:08:54,850
 They're all part of our Continental Army,

60
00:08:54,990 --> 00:08:56,590
 and I am Commander-in-Chief.

61
00:08:56,790 --> 00:08:59,110
 They're under my orders, and you are demoted, Colonel.

62
00:08:59,270 --> 00:09:00,870
 You don't deserve such a high rank.

63
00:09:01,070 --> 00:09:03,790
 You will return to your land, but not as a Colonel.

64
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:05,840
 We don't need your kind here.

65
00:09:07,290 --> 00:09:10,660
 General, I finally just happened to notice when I signed on

66
00:09:10,660 --> 00:09:12,660
 it was for three months, and it's up today,

67
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:14,660
 so I'll be going back home, General.

68
00:09:15,060 --> 00:09:15,490
 Bye.

69
00:09:19,050 --> 00:09:28,070
 Well, it really did happen that way.

70
00:09:28,350 --> 00:09:30,230
 There wasn't a regular army, not yet.

71
00:09:30,580 --> 00:09:33,580
 Not many weapons and virtually no money, and no money to buy any.

72
00:09:33,700 --> 00:09:36,420
 The English could land as many men and cannons as they like,

73
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:40,360
 and yet the Continental Army chalked up a number of successes, freeing Boston.

74
00:09:40,950 --> 00:09:45,870
 It was with a certain optimism that the Continental Congress reconvened in 1776.

75
00:09:47,820 --> 00:09:50,660
 It is a splendid document.

76
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:54,540
 It truly reflects the yearnings for liberty of the we Americans.

77
00:09:55,180 --> 00:09:56,320
 Here you are, John Adams.

78
00:09:56,940 --> 00:09:57,540
 Your turn.

79
00:09:57,540 --> 00:10:02,050
 Richard Henry Lee will now read an article which he helped to frame.

80
00:10:02,530 --> 00:10:05,410
 We hold these truths to be self-evident, that all men are created equal,

81
00:10:05,530 --> 00:10:08,590
 that they are endowed by their creator with certain unalienable rights,

82
00:10:08,670 --> 00:10:11,630
 and that among these are life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.

83
00:10:12,120 --> 00:10:15,140
 That to secure these rights, governments are instituted among men,

84
00:10:15,460 --> 00:10:17,800
 deriving their just powers from the consent of the governed.

85
00:10:18,120 --> 00:10:21,240
 That whenever any form of government becomes destructive of these ends,

86
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:26,140
 it is the right of the people to alter or abolish it and to institute new governments.

87
00:10:27,050 --> 00:10:30,490
 Laying its foundation on such principles and organizing its forms,

88
00:10:31,020 --> 00:10:36,450
 as to them shall seem most likely to affect their safety and their happiness.

89
00:10:41,560 --> 00:10:55,330
 Washington wasn't there to vote on the Declaration of Independence.

90
00:10:55,510 --> 00:10:58,910
 No, he was in New York on the eve of a massive English attack.

91
00:11:00,770 --> 00:11:04,150
 Over 30,000 redcoats.

92
00:11:04,350 --> 00:11:06,130
 It will be difficult to defend the city.

93
00:11:07,570 --> 00:11:10,510
 We must see to it that our men stand and fight well.

94
00:11:11,640 --> 00:11:14,810
 There are not many of us, and we have so few weapons.

95
00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:19,490
 I'll see what can be done.

96
00:11:19,490 --> 00:11:41,950
 In symbols of his men, Washington stood his ground, a perfect target for the English.

97
00:11:55,420 --> 00:12:04,690
 Any general is a friend. Well, here it is, a genuine Pennsylvanian rifle.

98
00:12:04,970 --> 00:12:10,040
 And with these, you hit a rabbit right between the eyes at a hundred paces, and soldiers as well.

99
00:12:10,490 --> 00:12:11,050
 No, general?

100
00:12:11,840 --> 00:12:16,240
 I thank you and your friends. It's with men of your caliber that will win our independence.

101
00:12:16,820 --> 00:12:18,400
 And you will be chief instructor.

102
00:12:19,150 --> 00:12:21,980
 And you, you'll be regimental commander.

103
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:27,720
 General, we can't put out the fire. It's burning all of New York. Well, what can we do?

104
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:33,760
 We'll do nothing to extinguish the flames. We would do well to burn the city down.

105
00:12:33,760 --> 00:12:37,540
 It will slow the English in their march and deprive them of winter quarters.

106
00:12:37,920 --> 00:12:39,980
 Providence has once again come to our assistance.

107
00:12:40,180 --> 00:12:41,160
 We must take advantage.

108
00:12:41,540 --> 00:12:44,840
 We shall retreat to the north if the redcoats don't trap us first.

109
00:12:45,790 --> 00:12:48,650
 There, too, they needed Providence to take a hand.

110
00:12:50,580 --> 00:12:53,560
 And this time in the form of a charming young woman.

111
00:12:54,940 --> 00:13:00,700
 General, come in and rest a while.

112
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:02,800
 I'm sure you're tired and need some refreshment.

113
00:13:07,950 --> 00:13:18,580
 Providence arranged things very well, delaying General Howe's advance by two days.

114
00:13:18,580 --> 00:13:34,040
 Battled continuously while retreating, and we've lost a third of our men. This can't go on.

115
00:13:34,260 --> 00:13:36,700
 There's only one way. We need a victory and right.

116
00:13:37,420 --> 00:13:42,430
 On Christmas night, 1776, Trenton on the Delaware River was attacked.

117
00:13:44,830 --> 00:13:52,430
 It was total surprise. Suffering very few losses, General Washington finally attained the victory that he needed so very badly.

118
00:13:54,010 --> 00:13:57,890
 You mean with the whole army, the English let General Washington beat them?

119
00:13:57,970 --> 00:14:00,930
 Naturally, they immediately set off after Washington and his men.

120
00:14:00,930 --> 00:14:02,850
 However, Washington had prepared for this.

121
00:14:03,110 --> 00:14:05,090
 At Princeton, he outwitted them, and besides,

122
00:14:05,210 --> 00:14:08,190
 the manuals of war they were using said never wage war in winter.

123
00:14:10,390 --> 00:14:19,390
 Meanwhile, back in London...

124
00:14:19,390 --> 00:14:24,090
 Excellency, Lord Germain, Secretary of State for the Colonies,

125
00:14:24,170 --> 00:14:27,210
 presents respects to the Earl of Sandwich, First Lord of the Admiralty.

126
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:42,560
 Just a second, my dear fellow. Be with you straight away.

127
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:45,500
 But, dash, don't remain stuck to the floor. Come here.

128
00:14:46,160 --> 00:14:49,090
 Allow me to slice a delicious morsel of this exquisite ham.

129
00:14:49,590 --> 00:14:53,350
 Quite so. However, my lord, I wish to inform you about the American Colonies.

130
00:14:53,350 --> 00:14:57,670
 I am now going to make history. You realize that, Jermaine? What?

131
00:14:58,500 --> 00:15:01,720
 Well...

132
00:15:01,720 --> 00:15:07,370
 James, would you bring me a lettuce leaf and one of those new fruits from the colonies?

133
00:15:07,730 --> 00:15:09,030
 Would it be a tomato?

134
00:15:09,430 --> 00:15:11,510
 A tomato, that's right, a tomato.

135
00:15:12,510 --> 00:15:17,910
 My dear sir, I suppose you are aware that our colony is being rather a bore on the other side of the Atlantic.

136
00:15:18,270 --> 00:15:20,490
 I was thinking perhaps we might...

137
00:15:20,490 --> 00:15:41,340
 Taste a morsel and then you tell me what you think.

138
00:15:47,090 --> 00:15:50,380
 Superb idea, truly. A most splendid repast. Very good.

139
00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:51,580
 Ah, what did I tell you?

140
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:53,920
 One would name such a delicacy.

141
00:15:54,080 --> 00:15:56,990
 It's marginal, uh, basket meal.

142
00:15:57,510 --> 00:15:59,650
 Oh, no, there is no basket here.

143
00:16:00,410 --> 00:16:03,150
 And what do you say to calling it a quick snack?

144
00:16:03,560 --> 00:16:04,920
 No, no, much too vulgar.

145
00:16:06,120 --> 00:16:07,300
 Of course, it's a sandwich.

146
00:16:07,700 --> 00:16:09,020
 It's a perfect name.

147
00:16:09,240 --> 00:16:10,340
 Ah, yes, why not?

148
00:16:10,420 --> 00:16:12,480
 I always said I would go down in history.

149
00:16:12,620 --> 00:16:14,440
 Now I will, and for my own creation.

150
00:16:16,270 --> 00:16:18,070
 I'm quite late.

151
00:16:18,110 --> 00:16:18,730
 I must go now.

152
00:16:18,730 --> 00:16:21,850
 Only there was a, yes, something I wanted to tell you about.

153
00:16:22,010 --> 00:16:22,950
 What was it now?

154
00:16:22,950 --> 00:16:24,230
 Oh, don't worry, old man.

155
00:16:24,310 --> 00:16:25,810
 You come and see me whenever you like.

156
00:16:25,950 --> 00:16:28,530
 We'll have a nice chat over a sandwich or two, all right?

157
00:16:28,910 --> 00:16:34,030
 Meanwhile, there in London, the brilliant General Bourgogne was devising an infallible plan.

158
00:16:34,050 --> 00:16:36,710
 I shall leave my 12,000 men, plus our loyalists, as well as Indians,

159
00:16:36,810 --> 00:16:40,850
 and I shall skirt Lake Champlain, moving along its shoreline to bring me as far as Albany.

160
00:16:40,970 --> 00:16:45,530
 Meanwhile, let Colonel St. Leger create a diversion before joining us through the Mohawk Valley.

161
00:16:46,060 --> 00:16:49,740
 General Howe will leave New York, going up the huts and bringing his troops to Albany.

162
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:51,440
 And then the trap is set.

163
00:16:51,560 --> 00:16:54,540
 And with all their communications out, there is only one option.

164
00:16:54,540 --> 00:16:56,820
 and that will be surrender, and this will crush the rebellion.

165
00:16:57,140 --> 00:16:58,140
 I want it clearly understood.

166
00:16:58,420 --> 00:17:00,720
 We will commit no acts of savagery, scalping not permitted.

167
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:02,860
 You are to conduct yourselves in a civilized manner.

168
00:17:03,220 --> 00:17:03,900
 You understand that?

169
00:17:04,150 --> 00:17:06,320
 You fight in the name of the British sovereign.

170
00:17:07,660 --> 00:17:09,800
 Well, at first, everything went according to plan.

171
00:17:10,220 --> 00:17:11,660
 Ticonderoga Fort was quickly taken.

172
00:17:17,310 --> 00:17:24,360
 But the campaign went from bad to worse,

173
00:17:24,360 --> 00:17:27,560
 as Burgoyne, who stubbornly and foolishly followed the military manual

174
00:17:27,560 --> 00:17:28,960
 rather than adapt to the land,

175
00:17:29,280 --> 00:17:32,660
 found that the Americans knew how to fight in that terrain, and he didn't.

176
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:42,620
 The English weren't having a good time of it.

177
00:17:43,100 --> 00:17:47,360
 In one month, the English had advanced 30 miles and losses were heavy.

178
00:17:47,760 --> 00:17:49,310
 Food stocks were running low.

179
00:17:50,270 --> 00:17:55,630
 Surrounded at Saratoga by American forces, far outnumbered, General Burgoyne surrendered.

180
00:18:00,210 --> 00:18:05,610
 General, keep your sword. Return to England with your men.

181
00:18:05,710 --> 00:18:07,870
 We will assist your departure in any way we can.

182
00:18:07,870 --> 00:18:12,920
 The quicker you're away, the better. That way we won't have to provide you any food.

183
00:18:13,240 --> 00:18:16,580
 General Gates, General Arnold, I appreciate your extreme generosity.

184
00:18:24,600 --> 00:18:28,440
 Sir, France is now officially at war with England at your side.

185
00:18:28,740 --> 00:18:29,980
 Mr. Franklin, and we agree.

186
00:18:30,220 --> 00:18:32,180
 No separate peace treaty with Great Britain.

187
00:18:32,340 --> 00:18:33,940
 Agreed, monsieur Vergine.

188
00:18:34,080 --> 00:18:35,440
 Believe me, you can count on me.

189
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:39,180
 The gratitude of the American people toward the French will be eternal.

190
00:18:39,400 --> 00:18:40,420
 We'll remember you.

191
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:47,850
 Two Frenchmen distinguished himself, fighting for the Americans, the Marquis de Lafayette.

192
00:18:48,400 --> 00:18:51,090
 And now forward together, my friends, it is for liberty we fight.

193
00:18:52,570 --> 00:18:56,130
 Attack and grant them no quarter attack.

194
00:18:56,130 --> 00:19:01,920
 On the ground, the situation was far from brilliant.

195
00:19:02,550 --> 00:19:08,370
 General Howe found that more gratifying to take Philadelphia rather than come to Burgoyne's rescue.

196
00:19:25,250 --> 00:19:26,570
 Ayette was in great danger.

197
00:19:38,060 --> 00:19:43,200
 1777 to 78 was almost unbearable on the plateau of Valley Forge.

198
00:19:43,280 --> 00:19:48,320
 The Americans were short of everything, food and clothing and weapons and tents and even firewood.

199
00:19:48,320 --> 00:20:00,560
 And money to purchase those was truly lacking.

200
00:20:01,510 --> 00:20:03,260
 January 1st that year.

201
00:20:07,810 --> 00:20:13,170
 While you are firing, you others in back, you reload and that way no break.

202
00:20:13,250 --> 00:20:14,230
 You begin to understand me.

203
00:20:14,270 --> 00:20:16,870
 Yeah, you load when the other one is shooting, yeah, you understand that, yeah?

204
00:20:16,990 --> 00:20:18,810
 You must understand, you load and shoot.

205
00:20:19,730 --> 00:20:22,550
 Well, General Von Steuben, tell me how it's going.

206
00:20:22,650 --> 00:20:25,370
 Yeah, good, good. You have only to seat the men now, General.

207
00:20:25,490 --> 00:20:28,450
 I present your army, your new continental army.

208
00:20:29,410 --> 00:20:48,990
 Monmouth was a complete rout, mostly because of the cowardice of General Lee.

209
00:20:49,330 --> 00:20:51,490
 But the enemy forces still remain terribly strong.

210
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:53,860
 Their resources are far superior to ours.

211
00:20:54,120 --> 00:20:56,780
 But, General, France remains your ally. Don't abandon hope.

212
00:20:56,780 --> 00:21:05,500
 Yes, but hope was thin on the ground. Whipped up by loyalist Indians, went on the attack, and many lives were lost, including women and even children.

213
00:21:06,710 --> 00:21:13,110
 A shadow was cast. Each learned to hate the other. Attitudes toward Indians were to be set for generations.

214
00:21:13,830 --> 00:21:21,010
 Then the fall of Charleston. That was the major defeat in the war and was to cost the Americans the greater part of their army. Was this to be the end?

215
00:21:22,020 --> 00:21:25,630
 Is there any hope? It's one defeat after another.

216
00:21:26,560 --> 00:21:28,980
 All the battles that were lost, didn't they win some?

217
00:21:28,980 --> 00:21:31,080
 Oh, yes, but Camden was another defeat.

218
00:21:31,570 --> 00:21:36,250
 It was a big one, and even though Washington was a man who could take defeat in his stride,

219
00:21:36,310 --> 00:21:39,730
 he was upset over Benedict Arnold, who defeated Burgoyne at Saratoga.

220
00:21:39,870 --> 00:21:44,500
 I agree. For 20,000 pounds, Major Andre, tonight I will give my troops leave,

221
00:21:44,580 --> 00:21:48,840
 and you will occupy with your men an undefended fortress I give you, West Point.

222
00:21:50,200 --> 00:21:56,040
 General, Major Andre has admitted everything.

223
00:21:56,470 --> 00:21:58,810
 General Arnold has fled to the English. What are your orders?

224
00:21:59,400 --> 00:22:01,840
 You will hang Major Andre at once.

225
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:03,600
 He asks to be shot like a soldier, sir!

226
00:22:04,160 --> 00:22:06,900
 No, he will be an example. Hang him as a traitor!

227
00:22:07,080 --> 00:22:07,280
 General!

228
00:22:08,100 --> 00:22:12,140
 And who can we trust? Tell me, who can we trust, my friend Lafayette?

229
00:22:13,030 --> 00:22:16,230
 Yes, it was a sad year back in 1780.

230
00:22:16,390 --> 00:22:20,430
 That was coming to what appeared to be a tragic and even hopeless end.

231
00:22:21,270 --> 00:22:23,370
 The same, the Americans won, right?

232
00:22:23,510 --> 00:22:25,150
 Yes, how did they manage to win?

233
00:22:25,750 --> 00:22:29,390
 Well, it was their courage and their perseverance and also France's help.

234
00:22:29,590 --> 00:22:31,150
 But the war wasn't, uh...

235
00:22:31,150 --> 00:22:33,550
 Oh, I'm sorry, I don't want to disturb you. I'm looking...

236
00:22:33,550 --> 00:22:37,590
 Oh, General! General Washington, a powerful French fleet is on its way.

237
00:22:38,260 --> 00:22:42,340
 It's commanded by Admiral de Grasse. At last, we will have forces to attack.

238
00:22:43,550 --> 00:22:47,550
 General Rochambeau, here's the situation in Connecticut, right near New York.

239
00:22:47,770 --> 00:22:54,550
 We can now deploy there 9,000 of our Americans and your 8,000 excellent French soldiers.

240
00:22:54,850 --> 00:22:56,430
 They are all at your orders, General.

241
00:22:57,250 --> 00:22:58,140
 What's our tactic?

242
00:22:58,140 --> 00:23:01,740
 Attack Clinton at New York, where he commands 8,000 soldiers.

243
00:23:02,120 --> 00:23:05,300
 Or go to the south, to Yorktown, to confront Cornwallis' 9,000.

244
00:23:05,840 --> 00:23:06,940
 - Which?

245
00:23:06,940 --> 00:23:10,660
 - Well, since Admiral De Grasse is heading for Chesapeake,

246
00:23:10,660 --> 00:23:14,220
 I would say that we go to Yorktown, if you agree, General.

247
00:23:14,220 --> 00:23:16,740
 - Yes, then we'll have them in our pincers,

248
00:23:16,740 --> 00:23:18,480
 but Yorktown's a long way.

249
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:21,900
 500 miles with cannons and baggage, ammunition.

250
00:23:21,900 --> 00:23:24,120
 It will be a hard journey, but we will make it.

251
00:23:32,600 --> 00:23:36,170
 - Sometimes I tell you, pay attention.

252
00:23:49,730 --> 00:23:51,230
 - There is yours, General Washington,

253
00:23:51,230 --> 00:24:01,950
 to set off the signal.

254
00:24:01,950 --> 00:24:07,790
 - Taken out, they block our advance.

255
00:24:07,790 --> 00:24:09,710
 Here we are at your orders, General Washington.

256
00:24:09,810 --> 00:24:10,910
 Here were all of us volunteers.

257
00:24:11,110 --> 00:24:12,350
 Don't worry, General, will do.

258
00:24:12,550 --> 00:24:15,230
 Your country is proud of you men, but watch your step.

259
00:24:45,690 --> 00:24:48,970
 19th, 1781, this was Yorktown.

260
00:24:53,040 --> 00:25:00,890
 After their surrender at Yorktown, the English still had many soldiers in America.

261
00:25:01,390 --> 00:25:03,360
 Still, they had no more will to fight.

262
00:25:03,360 --> 00:25:09,750
 The treaty guaranteeing the independence of the American colonies was signed in Paris in 1783.

263
00:25:10,150 --> 00:25:12,930
 And in 1789, just prior to the French Revolution,

264
00:25:14,100 --> 00:25:19,810
 George Washington was sworn in as the first president of the United States of America.

